\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Securing Open Sound Control (OSC) over UDP:
 A Lightweight HMAC-Based Authentication Scheme for Embedded Systems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Paul V.
 Miller
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This project presents a lightweight authentication layer for transmitting OSC (Open Sound Control) packets over UDP,
 using HMAC and sequence-based replay protection.
 The approach is designed for resource-constrained embedded controllers that operate in live performance or real-time musical applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
OSC
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
OSC (Open Sound Control) is widely used in computer music and interactive systems for sending live performance data.
 Very frequently the data is some musical control information (such as frequency or velocity) that is produced by a human interface.
 Many OSC software controllers are available online such as TouchOSC or Protokol;
 frequently people install these on a so-called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

smart
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

-phone or tablet.
 These applications frequently allow the user to design a custom interface that can include virtual buttons,
 sliders,
 knobs,
 or other kinds of control surfaces that produce data for a synthesizer or some other kind of application.
 A typical setup is shown below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename touchosc-photo.jpg
	lyxscale 10
	scale 60

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
TouchOSC with custom interface (image from TouchOSC's web site)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is also possible to build one's own physical controllers out of hardware components such as sliders,
 buttons,
 potentiometers,
 or even more sophisticated sensors (e.g.
 LSM9DS1 or BNO055).
 A small inexpensive microcontroller such as a Raspberry Pi Pico W can then format the data into an OSC message and send it through a wired or wireless connection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
OSC Encoding:
 The Details
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The OSC protocol uses a hierarchical address space in which each message includes an address pattern that identifies its destination on the receiving end,
 within a tree-like namespace (somewhat like a hierarchical filesystem structure).
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wright2002osc"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 The idea behind OSC was to improve upon the 1983 MIDI standard which,
 though useful in many cases,
 is limited to 7-bit values 0-127.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The most significant bit in MIDI (the 8th bit) distinguishes status bytes from data bytes,
 allowing the system to differentiate between 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

starting a new message
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (status byte) and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

this byte is a continuation of the current message
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (data).
 The designers of MIDI understood that there would probably only be 127 discrete values for pitch and volume,
 so they left it at that.
 This corresponds (very) roughly to the number of keys on the modern Steinway piano (88).
 The MIDI standard cannot natively send other kinds of performance data such as floating-point numbers or strings.
\end_layout

\end_inset

OSC is designed to work well with patching environments such as Max/MSP and Pd,
 because its message tree structures can be very easily separated using the 
\family typewriter
[route]
\family default
 object common in most patchers.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
While OSC has been widely adopted since its inception,
 it is not without critics.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "summer2012loveMIDI"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An OSC message can mix data types such as integer and float:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

/controller/move/ 60 0.75 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

legato
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{verbatim}
\end_layout

\end_inset

The command above sends a message to the address 
\family typewriter
/controller/move
\family default
 with three arguments:
 60,
 0.75,
 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

legato
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
It is something like sending parameters to a function:
 
\family typewriter
move(60,
 0.75,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

legato
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

)
\family default
.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This is the format that most naive users see.
 But OSC messages must also also include a positional tag that stipulates the data types being used in the correct order.
 For the message above,
 we need:
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

,ifs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{verbatim}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This positional tag stipulates that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the first datum is an integer,
 the second is a float and the third is a string
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The type tag always begins with a comma.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
There are several OSC type tags in the protocol specs.
 To summarize:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\series bold
numeric
\series default
:
 i,
 f,
 h,
 d (integer,
 float,
 8-byte int,
 8-byte float
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\series bold
text/binary
\series default
:
 s,
 b:
 string,
 blob (a binary blob can be of arbitrary length,
 and can transmit audio chunks,
 etc.)
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\series bold
special
\series default
:
 T,
 F,
 N,
 I:
 these do not have any corresponding data bytes – the type tag alone carries information (True,
 False,
 Null,
 Infinity)
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\series bold
structural
\series default
:
 t,
 m,
 r (timetag,
 MIDI,
 color in a 4-byte RGBA format!)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 To send the control message above,
 we must do the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
First encode the address string in hexadecimal 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
/controller/move
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which is null-terminated and padded to a multiple of 4 bytes (in this particular case we have 16 bytes for the string,
 plus 1 for the null termination and then 3 more for padding,
 resulting in a final payload of 20 bytes);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Next,
 encode the type tag string 
\family typewriter

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

,ifs
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\family default
,
 which must begin with a comma.
 It is also null-terminated and padded to a multiple of 4 bytes (in this specific case we have a four-byte tag,
 which means we need 1 more byte for the null termination and then 3 more bytes for padding,
 resulting in 8 bytes);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Finally we can send the arguments:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Integers are 32-bit signed,
 and need to be big-endian (network byte order);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Floats are 32-bit IEEE 754 standard floating-point,
 and are also big-endian;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Strings such as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

legato
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are standard ASCII and must be null-terminated,
 and padded to 4 bytes,
 so 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

legato
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in OSC is 8 bytes.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
These standards decrease the potential ambiguity in sending OSC messages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Threat Model:
 Background
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sending OSC messages usually happens over UDP,
 because transmission needs to be quick and low-latency.
 UDP does not normally engage in TCP's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

three way handshake
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (SYN – SYN-ACK – ACK) nor is there a sequence number involved,
 nor does it retransmit lost packets.
 The logic behind using UDP is that in live performance,
 it is usually better just to drop a lost packet than to wait for it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 because UDP removes several mechanisms for authentication and state,
 it makes some attacks much easier.
 While it is possible to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

spoof
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 TCP packets and even to inject data under some circumstances,
 UDP doesn't have any connection state,
 sequence tracking or verification.
 So the receiver doesn't really know where a packet came from.
 This makes spoofing and injection attacks much easier to perpetrate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Threat Assessment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a typical performance or installation that utilizes OSC over WiFi systems,
 an attacker has to be on the same network as the victim.
 For open networks,
 gaining this kind of access is trivial.
 For closed networks,
 it is not easy to crack a router's WiFi password (particularly if it is encoded using WPA2 or WPA3).
 However,
 musicians and artists are frequently unaware of the security vulnerabilities that routers expose.
 For example,
 routers are often shared assets that pass fluidly from person to person,
 with several individuals aware of the password.
 Router passwords are frequently shared in a cooperative environment.
 The sticker on the router advertising the credentials may not be removed,
 boldly advertising the password to any 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

curious
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 passerby who snaps a photo of the tech setup.
 Consequently,
 an attack would most likely come from an insider wanting to disrupt a performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the router password is known,
 an attacker would still need to know the port number through which OSC data was being transmitted.
 In TCP and UDP,
 port numbers are 16 bits long.
 Certain ports are more frequently used for OSC transmission because of their prevalence in online tutorials:
 for example,
 most Max/MSP tutorials use ports 7000,
 8000 or 9000.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Another widely-used synthesis environment – SuperCollider – simply defaults to port 57120.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Although port scanning is easy for TCP services discovery,
 it is more difficult on UDP.
 Although UDP services may not respond to invalid probes,
 it remains feasible to scan all 65,536 ports in a short period of time;
 a single successful OSC injection would tell the attacker that they found the right port.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Distinct from but related to message injection,
 
\shape italic
flooding
\shape default
 is a second problem that frequently occurs even when legitimately sending OSC control codes.
 Too many messages arriving at the same time can overload the ability to process them all,
 causing sequencing and timing issues in audio applications.
 Message flooding could occur if an attacker captured encrypted OSC control codes and replayed them,
 overwhelming the victim.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are no native security features for sending OSC messages over UDP apart from the general features of WiFi routers.
 The protocol operates entirely on a trust model.
 Most artists using these systems are not trained in security measures.
 I have personally witnessed radically insecure setups at electronic music performances.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Breach of this trust model can easily result in a catastrophic attack.
 Injecting OSC messages can grind a performance completely to a halt.
 If the unfortunate performers were to restart,
 the security issues would be unresolved and the attack could resume unabated.
 The impact on a live performance could be enormous,
 potentially resulting in disruption,
 significant reputational harm,
 and audience dissatisfaction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary:
 a 
\shape italic
very realistic threat
\shape default
 could come from an attacker who already has another device on the WiFi router,
 and knows the port.
 A 
\shape italic
moderately realistic threat
\shape default
 could come from someone who is on the WiFi router but brute-forces the port number.
 A 
\shape italic
less realistic threat
\shape default
 would be an external attacker who cracks WiFi,
 then scans ports.
\end_layout

