\begin_layout Subsection
Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
System Architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The system included senders (Raspberry Pi Pico W microcontrollers);
 a low cost consumer-grade Netgear router,
 and a receiver (a Macintosh M1 or M4 running Python3 and Max/MSP 9.x).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Packet Structure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The packet consisted of a header (12 bytes) which had a version number,
 device ID,
 sequence number and payload length.
 The header was structured in the following manner:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Header:
 
\family typewriter
>BBIIH
\family default
 formatted string for 
\family typewriter
struct.pack()
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
struct.unpack()
\family default
 in Python:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
> = indicates big-endian byte order (for network transmission);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
B = 1 byte = version number;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
B = 1 byte = flags;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
I = 4 bytes = device id;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
I = 4 bytes = sequence number;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
H = 2 bytes = payload length.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
OSC Payload = 28 bytes
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
The shortest control string I was transmitting is
\family typewriter
 /controller/pot1
\family default
.
 This is 16 characters.
 But OSC has to be null-terminated,
 so this becomes 17 bytes.
 I padded this to 20 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
OSC's type tag string,
 e.g.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

,f
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This is 2 characters plus a null terminator,
 so 3 bytes.
 Padded to 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The float argument in OSC is a 32-bit float,
 which adds 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Altogether this sums to 28 bytes for the entire OSC payload.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Now let's examine what happens if we transmit the longest control string:
 
\family typewriter
/controller/button1
\family default
.
 This sums to 19 characters,
 plus a null terminator = 20.
 This is already a multiple of 4,
 so it doesn't need padding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The type tag string for this is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

,i
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which is 2 characters plus the obligatory null terminator,
 which (when padded) again yields 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
OSC integers are 32 bits,
 so this is 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The total payload size in this case is also 28 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If I were to have a control string such as 
\family typewriter
/controller/button10
\family default
,
 I would have to increase the payload length.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Finally we have the HMAC-SHA256 tag.
 A full SHA-256 hash is 32 bytes,
 but I truncated it to 16 (which still gives 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{128}$
\end_inset

 possible tags,
 which is probably secure enough).
 I decided to truncate the hash to save some overhead and improve latency,
 which is important in this project.
 The keyed hash is computed over the header AND the payload.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 12 + 28 + 16 = 56,
 the total size of each packet is always 56 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Authentication Mechanism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As mentioned above,
 this is HMAC-SHA256 over the header and the OSC payload,
 truncated to 16 bytes.
 The key is NOT transmitted and is shared out of band.
 Sharing the key is therefore the responsibility of the people implementing the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Replay Protection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This was implemented with monotonically increasing sequence numbers.
 The receiver gateway rejects duplicates or out-of-order packets (depending on policy).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Implementation Details
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the secured Pico,
 we need the following files:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
controller.py
