\begin_layout Subsection
Performance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I did an analysis of latency by making a parallel branch of the code.
 Latency was measured in two ways:
 first as a round-trip timing (RTT),
 and second as gateway processing overhead on the Mac (receiving end).
 The round-trip method was chosen because it is not possible to synchronize clocks on the Raspberry Pi Pico W and the Mac.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of analyzing 30 packets reveal much interesting information.
 I found that latency was almost the same for plain OSC and secure OSC.
 Plain RTT averaged 211.1 - 212.0ms for most samples,
 with one outlier at 310.8ms.
 Secure RTT also averaged 211.2 - 212.2ms for most samples,
 with one larger outlier at 512.8ms.
 This data allows me to conclude that the practical end-to-end RTT in the project is dominated by something other than HMAC verification – most likely,
 the latency in WiFi variability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A better indication of the actual cost of the security layer was gateway-side timing.
 Gateway-side timing is the time taken to parse,
 verify and forward secure OSC packets to the loopback after the Python script receives them from the OS.
 This includes the time it takes to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
extract version,
 flags,
 device_id,
 seq,
 payload_len;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
check the length of the data to make sure it conforms to expected_len;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
check the recovered HMAC tag to make sure it conforms to the expected tag (using 
\family typewriter
hmac.compare_digest()
\family default
...)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
enforce replay protection;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
and finally,
 forward the verified OSC string to the loopback with 
\family typewriter
forward_sock.sendto(payload,
 ('127.0.0.1',
 8000)
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notably,
 gateway-side timing explicitly 
\shape italic
does not 
\shape default
include the network transit time it takes the packet to move from the Pico to the Mac,
 or socket receive blocking time.
 The timer starts only 
\shape italic
after
\shape default
 we receive a packet from the OS socket:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{Verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

recv_sock.bind(('0.0.0.0',
 9000))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

data,
 addr = recv_sock.recvfrom(...)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{Verbatim}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the plain gateway-side timing,
 processing time averaged about 160.8
\begin_inset Formula $\mu s$
\end_inset

 over 30 packets,
 with a range of 97.2 – 210.0
\begin_inset Formula $\mu s$
\end_inset

,
 while in the secure gateway-side timing,
 processing averaged 218.6
\begin_inset Formula $\mu s$
\end_inset

 with a rage of 146.9 – 366.5
\begin_inset Formula $\mu s$
\end_inset

.
 So we can conclude that 
\series bold
the secure version adds about 58
\begin_inset Formula $\mu s$
\end_inset

 on average
\series default
 to the gateway overhead compared to the plain OSC.
 This is negligible in the parameters of this project.
 The results are summarized in the table below.
\end_layout

