\begin_layout Section
Discussion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This lightweight system for authenticating OSC over UDP worked well,
 added only an insignificant amount of latency,
 and survived three simulated attacks.
 When a user installs the Python code on the transmitting and receiving ends,
 the system's security increases dramatically.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The most significant weakness in the current system is that a longer OSC payload is not supported,
 because the OSC string is limited to 28 bytes.
 A design goal for a potential version 2.0 would be to make the OSC payload variable.
 It is the user who should determine the size of the OSC payload,
 not the system.
 A future version would respond to whatever length OSC string the user sent into it (within reason).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
My system provides 
\shape italic
integrity
\shape default
 through HMAC,
 
\shape italic
authenticity
\shape default
 through a shared key,
 but lacks confidentiality as there is no encryption.
 This was a purposeful design choice,
 as I did not want to increase latency by introducing an encryption/decrypion layer.
 However,
 noting that the cost of the current system is so low,
 adding encryption might be worth pursuing in the future.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although an attack on transmitting OSC information over UDP is unlikely,
 it is potentially catastophic in a sensitive performance environment.
 My system provides an added layer of security to defend against three possible attack vectors,
 dramatically increasing the overall system resilience.
\end_layout

